<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Empire of Kitara</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1500 CE (approx.) · capital Bigo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A semi-legendary kingdom of the Great Lakes, associated with the Chwezi dynasty and the great earthworks of Bigo in western Uganda.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Oral traditions of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Bunyoro</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-ankole">Ankole</a> and neighbouring kingdoms speak of Kitara, a vast realm ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chwezi">Chwezi</a>, demigod kings remembered as bringers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> culture and new crafts. Archaeologists link these memories with large settlements of the 13th to 15th centuries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntusi">Ntusi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bigo-bya-mugenyi">Bigo bya Mugenyi</a>, where kilometres of ditches enclosed cattle and dwellings.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kitara appears to have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-centred state, with herders and farmers living side by side and the ruler's power resting on cattle wealth and spiritual authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chwezi">Chwezi</a> are remembered as founders of a spirit cult, kubandwa, still practised in the region. How large or centralised the state really was is uncertain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Traditions say the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chwezi">Chwezi</a> vanished mysteriously and were succeeded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babiito-dynasty">Babito</a>, Luo-speaking newcomers from the north, who founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Kingdom of Bunyoro</a> around 1500. Bunyoro and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-ankole">Kingdom of Ankole</a> both claimed Kitara's heritage.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/empire-of-kitara.jpg" alt="Empire of Kitara" width="320" height="382"><figcaption>Image: original by MellonDorderivative by Ahiise2 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kitara_per_D._H._Apuuli.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1500 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bigo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uganda">Uganda</a>, <a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a>, <a href="/countries/rwanda">Rwanda</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kitara, Chwezi Empire, Bachwezi kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1400&amp;focus=empire-of-kitara" aria-label="Empire of Kitara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5580 2165 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Empire of Kitara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5900 2421L5865 2378"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5865 2378h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5900 2421h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5900 2421h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1400&amp;focus=empire-of-kitara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
